{"hash":"263512b55ad30731","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":85.555,"said":"Before you go back down, stand at this rail and look down the valley, the way you came. The Mandakini runs down there, past Gaurikund, to Sonprayag, where the road begins. Above Sonprayag, at about 1,980 metres, lies the village of Triyuginarayan, and in front of its temple a fire burns in a hearth. Tradition says it has burned since a wedding: the wedding of Shiva and Parvati. Parvati had won Shiva through long penance, and it was there, they say, that the two were married. Vishnu stood as Parvati's brother and gave her away, and Brahma was the priest. The couple walked their rounds about this fire, and it has been kept alive ever since. Pilgrims still feed it with wood and carry home a pinch of its ash as a blessing. And the place takes its name from it: Triyugi Narayan, for a fire fed through three yugas, three ages of the world. Some say this fire burns here at Kedarnath; it does not. It is down that valley, a short way off the road at Sonprayag, and many pilgrims stop there on their way up or down. If your road passes that way, go and see it. Jai Baba Kedar!\n\nDid you know? Triyuginarayan has four sacred kunds, named for Rudra, Vishnu, Brahma and Saraswati.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1079},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1095,"end":1179}],"words":[[0.098,0.373,0,6],[0.483,0.096,7,10],[0.591,0.192,11,13],[0.795,0.312,14,18],[1.12,0.445,19,23],[1.793,0.409,25,30],[2.213,0.144,31,33],[2.37,0.192,34,38],[2.574,0.312,39,43],[2.899,0.216,44,47],[3.127,0.192,48,52],[3.331,0.288,53,57],[3.632,0.084,58,61],[3.728,0.469,62,68],[4.377,0.144,70,73],[4.533,0.204,74,77],[4.75,0.132,78,81],[4.894,0.445,82,86],[6.274,0.12,88,91],[6.406,0.685,92,101],[7.103,0.385,102,106],[7.5,0.276,107,111],[7.788,0.3,112,117],[8.293,0.361,119,123],[8.666,0.649,124,133],[9.519,0.144,135,137],[9.675,0.769,138,147],[10.709,0.228,149,154],[10.95,0.096,155,158],[11.058,0.325,159,163],[1